  "details": "In many Unity versions i have exported my projects and never had this issue before, it only appear's to be happening with the beta unity we are using right now namely: \n\"2017.4.15f1 LTS\"\n\nYou can reproduce this bug simply by exporting your project \"While crunching all textures on highest quality and RGBA DXT5\" \"Billnear\", on occasion it will double up the size of the original export and sometimes even triple. Even if you have changed barely a thing on your recent build. \n\nI have linked it directly with crunch compression as i've done two individual tests, and the package without compression did not double up or triple up the total accumulated data's size.\n\nThe fix is a simple one, but may take a while for you to use your computer as it puts your processor through an extensive import and crunching process.\n\nIn the Old unity versions the library folder was used in replacement of .meta files, \"this was before unity 5\" nowadays we still use the library folder, however its mainly to compile a list of asset hashes and all that has been imported over gets somewhat stored their as a form of backup.\n\nIf you look at the properties regarding the size of the library folder in your project, it will measure up perfectly with the size of what's actually in your scene, so all should be fine. However.. crunch compression seem's to cause a slight issue in regards to the export file-size overall.\n\nThe easiest way to resolve this from my own experience is to:\n\n1: \"save your scene as\" store scene saved in a folder not in library Folder.\n\n2: Exit unity.\n\n3: Delete the library folder.\n\nThis will now cause unity to re-import all of what was in your scene previously and re-calculate all asset hashes again. It will take a while to import \"Especially if you have crunched all of that data\" but unity will resolve the issue with a long process of importing each individual asset while at the same time crunching each individual asset, thanks to project settings folder.\n\nOnce this is done and your back in unity you may notice a few errors you did not see before too in console, none of which are bad.. but they should of been listed before to begin with, but was not until all hashes were re-calculated.\n\n4: Load your scene file within the destination you \"Saved As\" and just like magic you are now done.\n\nYou can now export your project and it will be back to exactly its normal size and wont measure double or even sometimes triple over its overall size.\n\nIt has happened to me now since i first started using the beta, and exporting my builds as backups, my original export is 1.6gigs, and randomly it will become 6.8 gigs, which unity cannot possibly cope with as it is apparently over its limit, there are many posts online regarding this issue and Unity may have improved this in later builds but so far i have not found a better solution other than this way by my own hand.\n\n\n@Omega2955",
